WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:08.030
In order to build deep neural networks, one modification to the basic convolutional operation

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.390
that you need to really use is padding.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.790
Let's see how it works.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:17.790
What we saw in earlier videos is that if you take a 6x6 image and convolve it with

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:23.990
a 3x3 filter, you end up with a 4x4 output with a 4x4 matrix.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:29.150
And that's because the number of possible positions for your 3x3 filter, they're only

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:37.910
4x4 possible positions for the 3x3 filter to fit in your 6x6 matrix.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:43.870
And the math for this turns out to be that if you have an nxn image and convolve that

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:58.520
with an fxf filter, then the dimension of the output would be n-f plus 1 by n-f plus 1.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:07.600
And in this example, 6 minus v plus 1 is equal to 4, which is why you wound up with a 4x4 output.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.560
So there are two downsides to this.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.880
One is that if every time you apply a convolutional operator, your image shrinks.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.480
So we've gone from 6x6 down to 4x4.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:21.880
Then you can only do this a few times before your image starts getting really small.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.040
Maybe it shrinks down to 1x1 or something.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.840
Maybe you don't want your image to shrink every time you detect edges or detect other features on it.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.680
So there's one downside.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.760
And the second downside is that if you look at the pixel at the corner of the edge,

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:43.840
this pixel is touchless used only in one of the outputs because it touches that 3x3 region.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.320
Whereas if you take a pixel in the middle, say this pixel,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.360
then there are a lot of 3x3 regions that overlap that pixel.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:02:01.440
And so it's as if pixels on the corners around the edges are used much less in the output.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.440
So you're throwing away a lot of the information near the edge of the image.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:13.120
So to solve both of these problems, both the shrinking output...

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.440
And when we build really deep neural networks,

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.560
you see why you don't want the image to shrink on every step.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.200
Because if you have maybe a 100 layer deep net,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.960
then if it shrinks a bit on every layer, then after 100 layers, you end up with a very small image.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.040
So that was one problem.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:38.280
The other is throwing away a lot of information from the edges of the image.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.760
So in order to fix both of these problems,

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.840
what you can do is before applying the convolutional operation,

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.880
you can pad the image.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:03:00.800
So in this case, let's say you pad the image with an additional border of one pixel or around the edges.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:09.000
So if you do that, then instead of a 6x6 image, you've now padded this to an 8x8 image.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.120
And if you convolve an 8x8 image with a 3x3 image, you now get back out.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.880
Now the 4x4, but a 6x6 image.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:23.640
And so you managed to preserve the original input size of 6x6.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.000
So by convention, when you pad, you pad it with zeros.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.400
And if p is the padding amount.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:37.360
So in this case, p is equal to 1.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.280
Because we're padding all around with an extra border of one pixel,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:54.350
then the output becomes n plus 2p minus f plus 1 by n plus 2p minus f by 1.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:04:02.550
So this becomes 6 plus 2 times 1 minus d plus 1 by the same thing.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:06.470
So 6 plus 2 minus d plus 1, that's equal to 6.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:12.990
So you end up with a 6x6 image that's preserving the size of the original input.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:21.390
So this green pixel actually influences all of these cells of the output.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:27.630
And so this effect of maybe not quite throwing away, but counting less the information from

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:33.030
the edge or from the corner of the edge of the image is reduced.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:38.270
And I've shown here the effect of padding the border with just one pixel.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.750
If you want, you can also pad the border with two pixels.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:45.910
In which case, I guess you add on another border here.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:51.510
And in fact, you can pad it with even more pixels if you choose.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:58.980
So I guess what I'm drawing here, this would be a pad of equal 2 or p equals 2.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:04.340
In terms of how much to pad, it turns out there are two common choices that I call

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:07.380
valid convolutions and same convolutions.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.940
I'm not sure these are great names, but in a valid convolution,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.560
this basically means no padding.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:22.720
And so in this case, you might have an n by n image convolved with an f by f filter.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:30.160
And this would give you an n minus f plus 1 by n minus f plus 1 dimensional output.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.680
So this is like the example we had previously from the previous videos where

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:39.000
we had an n by n image convolved with a 3 by 3 filter.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.580
And that gave you a 4 by 4 output.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:50.500
The other most common choice of padding is called the same convolution.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:06:00.110
And that means when you pad, so the output size is the same as the input size.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:04.190
So if you actually look at this formula, when you pad by p pixels,

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:08.710
then it's as if n goes to n plus 2p.

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:15.820
And then you have from the rest of this, minus f plus 1.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:22.460
So when you have an n by n image and a padding of a border of p pixels all around,

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:30.520
then the output size ends up being this dimension, n plus 2p minus f plus 1.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:36.280
And so if you want n plus 2p minus f plus 1 to be equal to 1, so

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:41.200
that the output size is the same as the input size, if you take this and

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.920
solve for, I guess n cancels out on both sides, and if you solve for p,

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:54.410
this implies that p is equal to f minus 1 over 2.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:59.050
So when f is odd, by choosing the padding size to be as follows,

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:03.010
you can make sure that the output size is the same as the input size.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:08.890
And that's why, for example, when the filter was 3 by 3,

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:12.810
as in the example in the previous slide, the padding that would make the output

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:21.730
size the same as the input size was 3 minus 1 over 2, which is 1.

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:30.050
And as another example, if your filter was 5 by 5, so if f is equal to 5,

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:34.250
then if you plug it into that equation, you find that the padding of 2 is

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:40.290
required to keep the output size the same as the input size when the filter

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:44.530
is 5 by 5.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:50.170
And by convention in computer vision, f is usually odd.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:55.660
It's actually almost always odd.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:08:03.260
And you rarely see even number filters, filter widths using computer vision.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.300
And I think there are two reasons for that.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:10.540
One is that if f was even, then you need some asymmetric padding.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:14.380
It's only if f is odd that this type of same convolution gives it a natural

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:16.900
padding, where you can pad the same dimension all around,

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:22.060
and then pad more on the left and pad less on the right, so something asymmetric.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:29.100
And then second, when you have an odd dimension filter, such as 3 by 3 or 5

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.020
by 5, then it has a central position.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:37.660
And sometimes in computer vision, it's nice to have a pixel.

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:43.660
You can call it a central pixel, so you can talk about the position of the filter.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:48.660
Maybe none of these is a great reason for using f to be pretty much always odd.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:53.660
But if you look at the computer vision literature, you see 3 by 3 filters are very common.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:56.900
You see some 5 by 5, 7 by 7s.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:00.500
And actually, sometimes later, we'll also talk about 1 by 1 filters

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:02.220
and when that makes sense.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:08.460
But just by convention, I recommend you just use odd number filters as well.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:12.540
I think that you can probably get just fine performance, even if you were to use

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:18.460
an even number value for f, but if you stick to the common computer vision convention,

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:22.700
I usually just use odd number f.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.940
So you've now seen how to use padded convolutions.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:31.940
To specify the padding for convolutional operation, you can either specify the value for p,

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:36.580
or you can just say that this is a valid convolution, which means p equals 0,

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:40.380
or you can say this is a same convolution, which means pad as much as you need

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:44.020
to make sure the output has the same dimension as the input.

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:45.020
So that's it for padding.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:49.180
In the next video, let's talk about how you can implement strided convolutions.
